course: world
lesson: 97
title: Festivals, holidays and traditions in the English-speaking world
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A festival or holiday is a special day when people remember something important, celebrate a season or share their beliefs. Many holidays have traditions — things people do the same way every year, like special food, songs or decorations.
explain: Some holidays come from religion: Christmas celebrates the birth of Jesus, and Easter remembers Jesus coming back to life. Others remember history: Independence Day in the USA marks 4 July 1776, and Remembrance Day on 11 November marks the end of the First World War in 1918.
explain: Some days are older than the countries themselves. Halloween on 31 October grew from Samhain, an old Celtic festival that marked the end of the harvest and the start of winter.
explain: Many English-speaking countries are home to people from all over the world, so they also celebrate Diwali, Hanukkah, Eid and the Lunar New Year. Learning about other people's festivals helps us understand our neighbours.
explain: The same holiday can feel very different in different places: in Australia and New Zealand Christmas comes in summer, so many families have a picnic or go to the beach.
fact: Christmas is on 25 December and celebrates the birth of Jesus.
fact: Easter is in spring in the north, and its date changes each year because it follows the Moon.
fact: Thanksgiving is on the fourth Thursday of November in the USA and the second Monday of October in Canada.
fact: Independence Day in the USA is on 4 July and remembers the Declaration of Independence of 1776.
fact: Halloween on 31 October grew from Samhain, an old Celtic festival at the end of the harvest.
fact: Bonfire Night on 5 November in Britain remembers a failed plot to blow up Parliament in 1605.
fact: St Patrick's Day on 17 March is Ireland's national day.
fact: Remembrance Day on 11 November marks the end of the First World War in 1918, and people wear red poppies.
fact: Anzac Day on 25 April remembers Australian and New Zealand soldiers.
fact: Canada Day is on 1 July and Australia Day is on 26 January.
fact: Diwali is the Hindu festival of lights, and Hanukkah is a Jewish festival of lights lasting eight nights.
fact: In the Southern Hemisphere Christmas falls in summer.
exercise: What is a tradition? | Something people do the same way every year | A new law passed by the government each spring | A holiday that happens only once | A game played only at school
exercise: On which date is Christmas Day? | 25 December | 1 January | 31 October | 24 November
exercise: What does Christmas celebrate? | The birth of Jesus | The end of a great war | The start of the harvest | A country's independence
exercise: Why does the date of Easter change every year? | It follows the Moon | It follows the school terms | The king chooses it each year | It moves one day later each year
exercise: Which country celebrates Independence Day on 4 July? | The United States | Canada | Ireland | The United Kingdom
exercise: On which day is Thanksgiving in the USA? | The fourth Thursday of November | The first Monday of September each year | The last Friday of October | The second Monday of October
exercise: Halloween grew out of which old festival? | Samhain, a Celtic festival at the end of the harvest | Diwali, a Hindu festival of lights | Thanksgiving, a harvest dinner in America | Hanukkah, a Jewish festival of lights
exercise: What does Bonfire Night on 5 November remember? | A failed plot to blow up Parliament in 1605 | The day the first bonfire was ever lit | The coronation of the first English king | The end of the First World War
exercise: Which country's national day is St Patrick's Day? | Ireland | Scotland | Wales | England
exercise: Why do people wear red poppies around 11 November? | To remember those who died in war | To welcome the start of spring | To celebrate the harvest being gathered | To mark the birthday of the king
exercise: What does Anzac Day remember? | Australian and New Zealand soldiers | The first ships that reached Australia | The founding of the city of Sydney | The end of a long summer drought
exercise: What is Diwali? | The Hindu festival of lights | A Jewish festival of eight nights | A Christian spring celebration | An old Celtic harvest festival
exercise: On which date is Canada Day? | 1 July | 4 July | 26 January | 17 March
exercise: Why is Christmas in summer in Australia? | Australia is in the Southern Hemisphere | Australia moves Christmas to June | Australia is closer to the Sun in winter | Australia has no winter at all
test: Ben's family in Melbourne eats Christmas lunch outside at the beach. Why is this normal there? | December is summer in the Southern Hemisphere | Christmas is in July in Australia | It never gets cold in any part of Australia at all | Australians do not celebrate Christmas
test: A family in Toronto and a family in Chicago both celebrate Thanksgiving. Which is true? | The Canadian family celebrates it about six weeks earlier | Both celebrate on exactly the same day | The American family celebrates it in October | Only the American family has Thanksgiving
test: Which pair matches a day with what it remembers? | 4 July — the American Declaration of Independence | 5 November — the end of the First World War | 11 November — a plot against Parliament | 25 April — the birth of Jesus
test: Which holiday is older than the English-speaking countries themselves? | Halloween, from the Celtic festival of Samhain | Canada Day, from the year 1867 | Independence Day, from the year 1776 | Australia Day, from the year 1788
test: Priya lights lamps at home for a festival of lights in autumn. Which festival is it most likely to be? | Diwali | Easter | St Patrick's Day | Anzac Day
test: Which statement is true? | Hanukkah is a Jewish festival lasting eight nights | Easter is always on the same date | Remembrance Day is in the spring | St Patrick's Day is Scotland's national day
test: Last year Easter was in late March. What can you say about next year's Easter? | It may fall on a quite different date | It will be on exactly the same date as last year | It will be in December | It will be exactly one week later
test: Why do many people in Britain, Canada and Australia fall silent on 11 November? | To remember people who died in wars | To listen for the first winter snow | To start the new school year | To mark the shortest day of the year
test: Why do English-speaking countries also mark Eid, Diwali and the Lunar New Year? | People from many backgrounds live there | These are the countries' oldest festivals | The law orders everyone to celebrate them | They were all invented in those countries
test: when's Bonfire Night | 5 November | 31 October | 11 November | 17 March
test: why's Christmas hot in Australia | In the Southern Hemisphere Christmas falls in summer | Australia moves Christmas to the summer | Australia is much closer to the Sun in December, so it's hot | It is only hot in the north of Australia
test: Mia says Easter's on the same date every year. true? | No, its date changes each year because it follows the Moon | Yes, it is always on 1 April | Yes, it is always the same Sunday | No, it changes because of the weather
test: my brother reckons Thanksgiving's in October in the USA. right? | No, in the USA it is the fourth Thursday of November | Yes, it is always the second Monday of October | Yes, but only every other year | No, it is on 4 July
exam: Ascertain the date upon which Independence Day is observed in the United States. | 4 July | 1 July | 25 December | 11 November
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the date of Easter changes each year. | It follows the Moon | It follows the Sun's eclipses | It is chosen by vote each year | It follows the harvest of wheat
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises Remembrance Day? | It marks the end of the First World War in 1918 | It marks the plot against Parliament in 1605 | It marks the Declaration of Independence of 1776 | It marks the birth of Jesus
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning Christmas in the Southern Hemisphere. | It falls in summer | It falls in winter | It falls in spring | It is not observed there
